{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7716965,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"lawn-care\",\"short_title\":\"lawn-care-schedule-houston-tx\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-07-27 00:23:10\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-07-27 00:23:10\",\"author\":\"Anna Kowalski\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A4b18497a39c24585bc9dde7ae0d9943fa.jpg\",\"title\":\"Houston Lawn Care Schedule: A Complete Season-by-Season Guide\",\"description\":\"Master your Houston lawn care schedule with our month-by-month guide covering mowing, fertilizing, weed control &amp; watering for a thriving Texas lawn.\",\"url\":\"/lawn-care/lawn-care-schedule-houston-tx\",\"body\":\"<h1>Houston Lawn Care Schedule: A Complete Season-by-Season Guide</h1>\\n\\n<p>Houston's unique climate presents both opportunities and challenges for homeowners trying to maintain a lush, healthy lawn. With long, hot summers, mild winters, high humidity, and the ever-present threat of both drought and heavy rainfall, keeping your grass green and thriving requires a strategic, well-timed approach. Whether you're working with <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine grass</a>, <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda grass</a>, <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia grass</a>, or <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\">Centipede grass</a>, timing your lawn care tasks correctly is the single most important factor in achieving the yard of your dreams.</p>\\n\\n<p>This comprehensive Houston lawn care schedule breaks down every task you need to perform throughout the year, organized by season, so you always know exactly what to do and when to do it. From pre-emergent herbicide applications in late winter to fall aeration and overseeding, we cover every step to keep your Houston lawn looking its absolute best.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Understanding Houston's Climate &amp; Growing Conditions</h2>\\n\\n<p>Before diving into the schedule, it's essential to understand the environmental factors that shape lawn care in the Houston area. Houston sits in USDA Hardiness Zone 9a, with average minimum temperatures ranging from 20°F to 25°F. The city receives approximately 50 inches of rainfall annually, with the heaviest rains typically falling in spring and early summer. However, prolonged dry spells are common from July through September, making irrigation management critical.</p>\\n\\n<p>The soil in the Houston metropolitan area varies significantly depending on your location. The Gulf Coastal Plain features heavy clay soils that drain slowly and compact easily, while areas further inland may have sandy loam that drains more quickly. This soil variability means that <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-soil-testing\\\">soil testing</a> should be one of your first priorities as a Houston homeowner. A soil test will reveal your pH level, nutrient deficiencies, and organic matter content, allowing you to customize your fertilization and amendment strategy.</p>\\n\\n<p>Houston's warm-season grasses follow a predictable growth cycle: they green up in spring, grow aggressively through summer, slow down in fall, and go dormant (or semi-dormant) in winter. Your lawn care schedule must align with this natural rhythm to be effective. Applying fertilizer too early in spring feeds weeds rather than your grass. Aerating during peak summer heat stresses your lawn. Timing is everything.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Spring Lawn Care in Houston (March Through May)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Spring is arguably the most critical season for Houston lawn care. This is when your warm-season grass breaks dormancy and begins its most vigorous growth period. Getting your spring routine right sets the tone for the entire growing season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>March: Waking Up Your Lawn</h3>\\n\\n<p>March marks the transition from dormancy to active growth in Houston. Your warm-season grass will begin greening up as soil temperatures consistently reach 55°F to 65°F. Here's what you need to focus on during this pivotal month:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Apply Pre-Emergent Herbicide:</strong> If you haven't applied a pre-emergent herbicide by early March, you're running out of time. Products containing prodiamine or dithiopyr create a chemical barrier in the soil that prevents crabgrass, sandbur, and other summer annual weeds from germinating. Apply when soil temperatures reach approximately 55°F at a 2-inch depth. Use a soil thermometer for accuracy rather than guessing based on air temperature.</li>\\n<li><strong>Begin Mowing:</strong> Once your grass shows consistent green growth and reaches about one-third taller than your recommended mowing height, it's time for the first mow of the season. For St. Augustine grass, set your mower to 3 to 4 inches. For Bermuda grass, aim for 1 to 2 inches. For Zoysia, target 1.5 to 2.5 inches. Never remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single mowing session, as this stresses the plant and invites disease.</li>\\n<li><strong>Sharpen Mower Blades:</strong> Dull mower blades tear grass rather than cutting it cleanly, creating ragged edges that turn brown and increase susceptibility to fungal diseases. Sharpen your blades at the start of every season and again mid-summer. A sharp blade produces a cleaner cut, reduces fuel consumption on gas mowers, and gives your lawn a noticeably healthier appearance.</li>\\n<li><strong>Assess Winter Damage:</strong> Walk your entire lawn and look for dead patches, areas of winter kill, or signs of disease such as large patch fungus (common in St. Augustine and Zoysia lawns during the transition period). Rake out dead thatch and debris from affected areas to allow sunlight and air to reach the soil surface.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>April: Fertilizing &amp; Weed Management</h3>\\n\\n<p>April is when your Houston lawn hits its stride. Soil temperatures are warm enough for consistent root growth, and the grass is actively pulling nutrients from the soil. This is the ideal window for your first major fertilization of the year.</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Apply Spring Fertilizer:</strong> Use a slow-release, nitrogen-rich fertilizer with a ratio such as 16-4-8 or 20-5-10. Slow-release formulations feed your lawn gradually over six to eight weeks, reducing the risk of fertilizer burn and minimizing nutrient runoff during Houston's frequent spring rains. Apply at a rate of approximately 1 pound of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet. Water the fertilizer in lightly after application to activate it and prevent leaf burn.</li>\\n<li><strong>Post-Emergent Weed Control:</strong> Any weeds that slipped past your pre-emergent barrier or emerged from perennial root systems should be spot-treated now. Use a selective herbicide labeled for your specific grass type. For St. Augustine lawns, products containing atrazine are effective against broadleaf weeds like dollarweed and clover. For Bermuda and Zoysia, look for herbicides with 2,4-D, dicamba, and mecoprop (MCPP). Always read the label carefully — using the wrong herbicide on St. Augustine or Centipede grass can cause severe damage.</li>\\n<li><strong>Monitor Irrigation:</strong> Houston's spring rainfall can be generous, but it's often inconsistent. Aim to provide your lawn with 1 to 1.5 inches of water per week, combining rainfall and irrigation. Invest in a rain gauge to track precipitation and avoid overwatering, which promotes shallow root growth and fungal diseases like <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/brown-patch-fungus\\\">brown patch</a>.</li>\\n<li><strong>Edge &amp; Define Beds:</strong> Crisp edges along sidewalks, driveways, and garden beds instantly elevate the appearance of your lawn. Use a powered edger or a sharp spade to create clean lines. This also prevents grass from creeping into flower beds and makes mowing easier throughout the season.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>May: Preparing for Summer Heat</h3>\\n\\n<p>May in Houston signals the approach of intense summer heat. Your lawn is growing rapidly, and your maintenance routine needs to keep pace.</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Increase Mowing Frequency:</strong> During peak growth in May, you may need to mow every five to seven days rather than the typical weekly schedule. Frequent mowing at the correct height encourages lateral growth and thickens your turf, which naturally crowds out weeds.</li>\\n<li><strong>Check for Chinch Bugs:</strong> Chinch bugs are a significant pest in Houston, particularly in St. Augustine lawns. Look for yellowing or browning patches in sunny areas, especially near sidewalks and driveways where heat radiates. Part the grass at the border between healthy and damaged areas and look for small black insects with white markings. If you find 20 or more chinch bugs per square foot, treatment with a labeled insecticide is warranted.</li>\\n<li><strong>Service Your Irrigation System:</strong> Before the brutal summer heat arrives, inspect every sprinkler head for proper operation. Look for clogged nozzles, misaligned heads spraying onto pavement, and leaks. Run each zone and observe coverage patterns. Adjust or replace heads as needed to ensure even water distribution across your entire lawn.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Summer Lawn Care in Houston (June Through August)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Summer is the most demanding season for Houston lawns. Temperatures regularly exceed 95°F, humidity is oppressive, and the combination of heat and moisture creates ideal conditions for both pests and fungal diseases. Your summer strategy should focus on maintaining your lawn's health through stress management rather than pushing aggressive growth.</p>\\n\\n<h3>June: Peak Growing Season Maintenance</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Mow High:</strong> Raise your mower deck to the highest recommended setting for your grass type during summer. Taller grass shades the soil, reducing evaporation, suppressing weed germination, and keeping root zones cooler. For St. Augustine, maintain 3.5 to 4 inches. For Bermuda, keep it at 1.5 to 2 inches. For Zoysia, aim for 2 to 2.5 inches.</li>\\n<li><strong>Water Deeply &amp; Infrequently:</strong> Switch to a deep watering schedule of 1 to 1.5 inches per week, applied in one or two sessions rather than daily light sprinklings. Deep watering encourages roots to grow deeper into the soil, where they access moisture reserves during dry periods. Water early in the morning, ideally between 4:00 AM and 8:00 AM, to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of fungal diseases that thrive when grass stays wet overnight.</li>\\n<li><strong>Apply Grub Preventative:</strong> June is the ideal time to apply a preventative grub control product containing imidacloprid or chlorantraniliprole. White grubs are the larvae of June beetles and other scarab beetles common in the Houston area. They feed on grass roots from mid-summer through fall, causing large brown patches that can be mistaken for drought stress. Preventative applications are far more effective than curative treatments applied after damage has occurred.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>July: Heat Stress Management</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Reduce Foot Traffic:</strong> During extreme heat, grass is already under significant stress. Minimize foot traffic on your lawn during July and August, particularly during the hottest part of the day. Heavy traffic on stressed grass can cause lasting damage and create bare spots that weeds will quickly colonize.</li>\\n<li><strong>Watch for Fungal Diseases:</strong> The combination of Houston's heat and humidity creates a perfect environment for fungal diseases like brown patch (Rhizoctonia solana), gray leaf spot, and take-all root rot. Brown patch appears as circular or irregular brown patches, often with a dark \\\"smoke ring\\\" border visible in the early morning. If you suspect a fungal disease, apply a preventive fungicide containing azoxystrobin or propiconazole. Avoid applying nitrogen-heavy fertilizers during active fungal outbreaks, as lush new growth is more susceptible to infection. For more information, see our guide on <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/identifying-lawn-diseases\\\">identifying and treating common lawn diseases</a>.</li>\\n<li><strong>Leave Grass Clippings:</strong> Mulching grass clippings back into the lawn during summer provides a natural source of nitrogen, helps retain soil moisture, and keeps soil temperatures lower. Contrary to popular belief, clippings do not contribute to thatch buildup when mowed regularly. If your mower has a mulching blade, use it. If clippings are excessive and clumping, you may need to mow more frequently or bag occasionally.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>August: Sustaining Through the Hottest Month</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Maintain Irrigation Vigilance:</strong> August is typically Houston's hottest and driest month. Your lawn may need up to 1.5 inches of supplemental water per week if rainfall is insufficient. Use a simple tuna can test: place several empty tuna cans across your lawn, run your sprinklers, and time how long it takes to fill them to the 1-inch mark. This helps you calibrate your irrigation system for accurate watering.</li>\\n<li><strong>Light Fertilization (If Needed):</strong> If your lawn shows signs of nitrogen deficiency (pale green or yellowing color) in August, apply a light dose of slow-release fertilizer at half the normal rate. Avoid heavy fertilization during peak heat, as this forces the grass to produce new growth when it should be conserving energy. A product with iron (chelated iron or iron sulfate) can green up your lawn without pushing excessive top growth.</li>\\n<li><strong>Plan for Fall Renovation:</strong> August is the time to assess your lawn's overall condition and plan any fall renovation projects. Identify thin or bare areas that will need attention. If you're considering <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/core-aeration\\\">core aeration</a>, schedule your equipment rental or professional service now, as fall is the ideal time for this task in Houston.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Fall Lawn Care in Houston (September Through November)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Fall is the second most important season for Houston lawn care. While warm-season grasses are slowing their top growth, they're still actively building root systems and storing energy for winter dormancy and the following spring's green-up. Proper fall care directly impacts how well your lawn survives winter and how quickly it recovers in spring.</p>\\n\\n<h3>September: Recovery &amp; Preparation</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Core Aeration:</strong> September is the prime window for <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/core-aeration\\\">core aeration</a> in Houston. Summer foot traffic, heavy clay soils, and frequent mowing all contribute to soil compaction, which restricts root growth, limits water infiltration, and reduces fertilizer effectiveness. Core aeration removes small plugs of soil from the lawn, creating channels for air, water, and nutrients to reach the root zone. For best results, aerate when the soil is moist but not saturated, and leave the soil cores on the lawn to break down naturally.</li>\\n<li><strong>Apply Fall Fertilizer:</strong> Approximately four to six weeks after your aeration, apply a fall fertilizer with a higher potassium content (the third number in the NPK ratio). Potassium strengthens cell walls, improves drought tolerance, and enhances cold hardiness — all critical for winter survival. A fertilizer with a ratio like 15-5-15 or 10-5-20 works well for Houston lawns in fall.</li>\\n<li><strong>Overseed with Ryegrass (Optional):</strong> If you want a green lawn throughout Houston's mild winter, overseeding with annual or perennial ryegrass is a popular option. This practice is most commonly done on Bermuda grass lawns, as Bermuda goes fully dormant and turns brown. St. Augustine and Zoysia lawns are generally not overseeded, as the ryegrass competes with these grasses during spring green-up and can delay recovery. If you choose to overseed, mow your Bermuda grass short (about 1 inch), rake to expose soil, spread ryegrass seed at 5 to 10 pounds per 1,000 square feet, and keep the soil consistently moist until germination occurs in seven to ten days.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>October: Weed Prevention &amp; Final Mowing</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Apply Fall Pre-Emergent Herbicide:</strong> This is one of the most important — and most overlooked — tasks in Houston lawn care. Fall pre-emergent application prevents cool-season annual weeds like annual bluegrass (Poa annua), henbit, chickweed, and lawn burweed from germinating in your lawn. Apply when soil temperatures drop to around 70°F, typically in early to mid-October in Houston. Products containing prodiamine or pendimethalin provide excellent control. This single application can dramatically reduce the number of weeds you battle throughout winter and early spring.</li>\\n<li><strong>Continue Mowing as Growth Slows:</strong> Your warm-season grass will continue growing through October, albeit at a slower pace. Continue mowing as needed, but you can gradually lower your mower height by about half an inch for the final few mowings of the season. This prevents the grass from matting down under winter moisture, which can promote snow mold and other cool-season diseases.</li>\\n<li><strong>Manage Fallen Leaves:</strong> While Houston doesn't experience the dramatic leaf drop of northern climates, many trees in the area shed leaves throughout fall. Don't allow leaves to accumulate on your lawn, as they block sunlight, trap moisture, and create conditions favorable for fungal diseases. Mulch light leaf cover with your mower, or rake and remove heavier accumulations.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>November: Preparing for Dormancy</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Final Mowing:</strong> Your last mowing of the season should bring your grass to its recommended winter height — slightly shorter than summer height but not scalped. For St. Augustine, aim for about 2.5 to 3 inches. For Bermuda, 1 to 1.5 inches is appropriate. Scalping the lawn too short exposes the crown to cold damage and encourages weed invasion.</li>\\n<li><strong>Winterize Your Irrigation System:</strong> While Houston rarely experiences hard freezes that can burst pipes, it's wise to drain above-ground irrigation components and insulate any exposed valves or backflow preventers. A single hard freeze can cause costly damage to unprotected irrigation equipment.</li>\\n<li><strong>Clean &amp; Store Equipment:</strong> Drain fuel from your mower or add fuel stabilizer, change the oil, clean or replace the air filter, and store your equipment in a dry location. Proper off-season maintenance extends the life of your mower and ensures it's ready to go when spring arrives.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Winter Lawn Care in Houston (December Through February)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Winter in Houston is mild compared to most of the country, but that doesn't mean your lawn care responsibilities disappear entirely. This is a season for planning, maintenance, and selective intervention.</p>\\n\\n<h3>December &amp; January: Dormant Season Tasks</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Minimize Traffic on Dormant Grass:</strong> Warm-season grasses are dormant during December and January. While the grass appears brown or pale green, the crown and root systems are alive but vulnerable. Excessive foot traffic during dormancy can damage crowns and create thin spots that won't recover until spring. If you've overseeded with ryegrass, traffic on the ryegrass is fine, but try to avoid concentrating wear on the same areas repeatedly.</li>\\n<li><strong>Control Winter Weeds:</strong> Cool-season weeds like henbit, chickweed, and annual bluegrass are actively growing during Houston's winter. Spot-treat these weeds with a selective herbicide appropriate for your grass type. On dormant Bermuda grass (not overseeded), you can use a non-selective herbicide like glyphosate to kill winter weeds without harming the dormant Bermuda — but exercise extreme caution and never use this approach on St. Augustine or Zoysia, which may not be fully dormant in Houston's mild winters.</li>\\n<li><strong>Service &amp; Upgrade Equipment:</strong> Winter is the perfect time to evaluate your lawn care tools and equipment. Replace worn mower blades, purchase a new <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/best-lawn-spreaders\\\">spreader</a> if yours is corroded, and stock up on supplies for spring. Buying fertilizer and herbicides during the off-season often means better prices and wider selection.</li>\\n<li><strong>Review &amp; Plan:</strong> Study your lawn's performance from the past growing season. What worked well? Where did you struggle with weeds, disease, or thin turf? Use this information to refine your lawn care plan for the coming year. Consider ordering a <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-soil-testing\\\">professional soil test</a> now so results arrive in time to guide your spring fertilization strategy.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>February: Early Spring Preparation</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Apply Late Winter Pre-Emergent:</strong> In Houston, crabgrass and other summer annual weeds can begin germinating as early as late February. Apply your spring pre-emergent herbicide between February 15 and March 1, depending on weather conditions. The traditional indicator is when soil temperatures at a 2-inch depth reach 55°F for several consecutive days. If you miss this window and weeds have already germinated, you'll need to rely on post-emergent treatments, which are less effective and more expensive.</li>\\n<li><strong>Scalp Dormant Bermuda (Optional):</strong> If you have a Bermuda grass lawn that was not overseeded, you can scalp it in late February to early March by mowing it very short (about 0.5 to 1 inch) and bagging the clippings. This removes dead thatch and dormant tissue, allowing sunlight to warm the soil more quickly and promoting earlier green-up. Do not scalp St. Augustine or Zoysia lawns, as this can severely damage these grass types.</li>\\n<li><strong>Sharpen Tools &amp; Prepare for Spring:</strong> Sharpen mower blades, edger blades, and any hand tools. Check your sprayer for leaks and clogged nozzles. Calibrate your spreader to ensure accurate application rates. Having everything in working order before the spring rush means you won't miss critical application windows.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Essential Tools &amp; Products for Houston Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Having the right tools and products on hand makes following this schedule significantly easier. Here are the essentials every Houston homeowner should invest in:</p>\\n\\n<h3>Must-Have Equipment</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Quality Rotary or Reel Mower:</strong> For St. Augustine and Zoysia, a rotary mower with adjustable height settings is ideal. For Bermuda grass maintained at lower heights, a reel mower provides a superior cut. Look for models with mulching capabilities and easy height adjustment.</li>\\n<li><strong>Broadcast or Drop Spreader:</strong> A broadcast spreader covers more ground quickly and is ideal for open lawn areas. A drop spreader offers more precision near garden beds and property lines. Many homeowners benefit from owning both. Calibrate your spreader at least once per season to ensure accurate application rates.</li>\\n<li><strong>Pump Sprayer or Hose-End Sprayer:</strong> For liquid herbicide and fungicide applications, a 1- to 2-gallon pump sprayer provides excellent control for spot treatments. For larger areas, a hose-end sprayer connects to your garden hose for broader coverage.</li>\\n<li><strong>Soil Thermometer:</strong> This inexpensive tool is one of the most valuable items in your lawn care arsenal. Soil temperature, not air temperature or calendar date, should drive your pre-emergent and fertilization timing.</li>\\n<li><strong>Rain Gauge:</strong> Tracking rainfall helps you adjust irrigation schedules and avoid overwatering, which is just as harmful as underwatering in Houston's clay soils.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Recommended Products by Season</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Spring Pre-Emergent:</strong> Prodiamine 65 WDG or Dimension (dithiopyr) for broad-spectrum weed prevention</li>\\n<li><strong>Spring Fertilizer:</strong> Slow-release granular fertilizer with 16-4-8 or similar NPK ratio, preferably with micronutrients including iron</li>\\n<li><strong>Summer Fungicide:</strong> Azoxystrobin-based products (such as Scotts DiseaseEx) for brown patch and other common Houston lawn diseases</li>\\n<li><strong>Grub Control:</strong> Imidacloprid-based products applied in June for preventative grub management</li>\\n<li><strong>Fall Pre-Emergent:</strong> Prodiamine or pendimethalin for cool-season weed prevention</li>\\n<li><strong>Fall Fertilizer:</strong> High-potassium formulation (such as 15-5-15) for winter hardiness</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>For more detailed product guidance, check out our reviews of the <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/best-lawn-fertilizers\\\">best lawn fertilizers</a> and <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/best-weed-killers\\\">best weed killers</a> for warm-season lawns.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Houston Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<h3>How often should I water my Houston lawn during summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>During Houston's summer months, aim to provide your lawn with 1 to 1.5 inches of water per week, applied in one or two deep watering sessions rather than daily light sprinklings. Water early in the morning, between 4:00 AM and 8:00 AM, to minimize evaporation and reduce disease risk. Use a rain gauge to account for rainfall, and adjust your irrigation accordingly. Signs that your lawn needs water include footprints that remain visible on the grass for more than 30 minutes, a bluish-gray color, and wilting or curling grass blades. Clay soils common in Houston hold moisture longer than sandy soils, so homeowners with heavy clay may need to water less frequently but should monitor for runoff during irrigation.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What is the best grass type for Houston's climate?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The best grass type for your Houston lawn depends on your specific conditions. <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine grass</a> is the most popular choice due to its excellent shade tolerance and lush appearance, making it ideal for yards with mature trees. <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda grass</a> thrives in full sun, tolerates heavy foot traffic, and recovers quickly from damage, making it excellent for active families. <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia grass</a> offers a beautiful, dense turf with good shade and sun tolerance but establishes slowly and requires more patience. <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\">Centipede grass</a> is a low-maintenance option that requires less fertilizer but is slower to recover from damage. Consider your yard's sun exposure, soil type, traffic levels, and desired maintenance commitment when choosing your grass type.</p>\\n\\n<h3>When should I apply pre-emergent herbicide in Houston?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Houston lawns benefit from two pre-emergent herbicide applications per year. The spring application should be made between mid-February and early March, when soil temperatures at a 2-inch depth reach approximately 55°F for several consecutive days. This prevents summer annual weeds like crabgrass and sandbur from germinating. The fall application should be made in early to mid-October, when soil temperatures drop to around 70°F, to prevent cool-season weeds like annual bluegrass (Poa annua), henbit, and chickweed. Soil temperature is a far more reliable indicator than calendar dates, as weather patterns vary from year to year. Invest in a soil thermometer and check temperatures in multiple areas of your lawn, as shaded areas warm more slowly than sunny spots.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How do I fix brown patches in my Houston lawn?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Brown patches in Houston lawns can have several causes, and correct diagnosis is essential for effective treatment. The most common causes include brown patch fungus (Rhizoctonia solana), which appears as circular brown areas with a dark border, often during periods of high humidity; chinch bug damage, which manifests as irregular yellowing and browning in sunny areas; drought stress, which causes uniform wilting and browning; and grub damage, where the grass pulls up easily because roots have been eaten. To diagnose, examine the affected area closely: look for insects at the soil line, check if roots are intact, and observe the pattern and timing of damage. Fungal diseases require fungicide treatment, insect damage requires targeted insecticides, and drought stress requires adjusted irrigation. If you're unsure of the cause, contact your local Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension office for a free or low-cost diagnostic service. Our guide on <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/identifying-lawn-diseases\\\">identifying lawn diseases</a> can also help you narrow down the problem.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Should I aerate my Houston lawn, and how often?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Yes, core aeration is highly beneficial for most Houston lawns, particularly those with heavy clay soil that compacts easily. Aeration should be performed once per year, ideally in September when warm-season grasses are still actively growing but the worst of the summer heat has passed. Core aeration removes small plugs of soil (typically 2 to 3 inches deep and 0.5 to 0.75 inches in diameter) from the lawn, relieving compaction and creating channels for water, air, and nutrients to penetrate the root zone. Signs that your lawn needs aeration include water pooling on the surface after irrigation, hard soil that resists a screwdriver probe, thin or slow-growing grass despite proper fertilization, and heavy foot traffic areas. After aeration, leave the soil cores on the lawn to break down naturally over one to two weeks. This is also an excellent time to apply <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-topdressing\\\">topdressing</a> with compost or sand to further improve soil structure.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Final Thoughts on Houston Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Maintaining a beautiful lawn in Houston requires consistency, patience, and a willingness to adapt your approach based on weather conditions and your lawn's response. This schedule provides a framework, but every lawn is unique. Pay attention to how your grass responds to different treatments, keep notes on what works and what doesn't, and don't be afraid to adjust timing based on the specific conditions in your yard.</p>\\n\\n<p>Remember that healthy soil is the foundation of a healthy lawn. Invest in regular <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-soil-testing\\\">soil testing</a>, build organic matter through mulching clippings and topdressing with compost, and avoid the temptation to over-fertilize. A lawn grown in healthy, well-structured soil with a robust root system will naturally resist weeds, tolerate drought, and recover from stress far more effectively than one that relies solely on chemical inputs.</p>\\n\\n<p>By following this Houston lawn care schedule and staying proactive throughout each season, you'll build a thicker, greener, more resilient lawn year after year. For more in-depth guidance on specific topics, explore our complete library of <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care\\\">lawn care guides</a> and <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types\\\">grass type profiles</a>.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-07-27 00:35:20\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}